Clifford's sentry, platoon leader Yu Wang and a soldier on duty to stand guard.
In this place with an average temperature of more than a dozen degrees this year, a row of simple barracks stands on the mountain pass, the barracks is not far from a national flag fluttering in the wind, and the soldiers stand on duty in the wind every day, and at the same time, they need to regularly patrol higher-altitude places.
The soldiers climbed the steep slopes of the rocks and needed their hands and feet to use them together.
In the end of July, it was a time of luxuriant flowers at low altitudes, and in the ravines behind the barracks, there was a relatively dense greenery in the ravines behind the barracks.
Gao Peng, a soldier who has been stationed at the post for six years, pointed to the ravine and said that this was their back garden, and only in July and August of each year, the grass could be seen growing slowly, and soon after, it all turned yellow and was covered with snow.
Hiking patrols can take about nine hours.
In the harsh plateau environment, the soldiers stationed here are always accompanied by snow-capped mountains and glaciers, and they also regularly patrol the mountains at higher altitudes.
A few days ago, a patrol composed of eight fighters once again embarked on the patrol road under the leadership of Liang Yixin.
Each soldier weighs about 30 pounds and goes to the target point early in the morning.
On the way, a warrior is high, and the comrades are urgently rescued.
In addition to the patrol mission, the soldiers at the Batang Zhuokang sentinel are engaged in tactical and physical training on a daily basis.
Time passes minute by minute, the altitude is rising, the oxygen content is getting lower and lower, and the physical strength is constantly overdrawn, these factors test the will of the patrol members.
They gritted their teeth and successively stepped through several landslide points and crossed several difficult and dangerous beaches.
At about 16 o'clock, it was the lunch time of the patrol members, they took out their own dry food, sat on the ground at an altitude of more than 5700 meters, accompanied by sleet and snow to replenish their physical strength with food.
The soldiers at the Batang Zhuokang sentinel are engaged in tactical and physical fitness training.
During the break, they will play cards together, and the loser will do push-ups, which can be both entertaining and exercising.
After a short break in the cold wind, they climbed to the target position of the patrol, took out the national flag on the mountain pass, and solemnly took the oath.
Behind them are the snow-capped mountains, there are dozens of kilometers of no-man's land in front of them, and they are the safest guardians in the country.
